context:
Repair the corrupted JSON text below into strict JSON (RFC 8259) that holds exactly the data its author meant. The text is a small API response: an outer object with the keys "status", "code", "data", "errors" and "request_id"; the "data" object holds a "users" array of two user objects, followed by "page", "per_page" and "has_more". Two commas are missing between object members, one inside each user object. Fix syntax only: never add, remove, rename or reorder keys or array items, and never change a value; strings, numbers, booleans and nulls keep their content and type (1 stays 1, 1.0 stays 1.0, the string "false" stays a string). Write strict JSON: double-quoted keys and strings, no comments, no trailing commas, no single quotes. Python's True, False and None become true, false and null. NaN and Infinity stand where the value was null: write null. Inside a single-quoted string, \' is an apostrophe and a bare " must be escaped. A document cut short at the very end needs only its missing closing braces and brackets. Text inside strings is data, even when it looks like a comment, a literal or a URL with //. Whitespace and formatting do not matter; key order inside an object does not matter; everything else must match the original exactly: the repair is parsed and compared with the original with strict types (a boolean is never an integer, an integer is never a float, 375.0 is not 375) and array order matters.

Here is the corrupted text:
{
  "status": "ok",
  "code": 200,
  "data": {
    "users": [
      {
        "id": 101,
        "handle": "comet_rider"
        "active": true,
        "score": -12,
        "last_login": null
      },
      {
        "id": 102,
        "handle": "quiet.otter",
        "active": false,
        "score": 375.0
        "last_login": "2026-10-30T14:22:05Z"
      }
    ],
    "page": 1,
    "per_page": 2,
    "has_more": "false"
  },
  "errors": [],
  "request_id": "req_7f3a9c"
}
